// @emit-error field access receiver is not a struct
// @hint redundant type annotation: `cell` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `c` is inferred as `{r: i32}`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `l` is inferred as `Circle[]`
// D63. A FLOOR PIN, not a decision — flip it when D50's floor lifts.
//
// A `for v in <Circle[]>` where `Circle` is a union ARM has no rep: `forInElemKind` has no
// `"variant"` arm, so `declareForInLocals` binds the loop var through the struct leg and
// there is no struct row for an arm. That is D50, and it is a LOUD emit reject.
//
// DECLARING AN EXACT LAYOUT TWIN USED TO TURN THAT FLOOR OFF. With `type Dot = { r: i32 }`
// present — never mentioned again, no value of it built — the same program was `vl check`
// rc 0 and "type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)", the family's
// two-heap-types-behind-one-placeholder signature. The bypass was `structIdxOfElemName`, the
// SLOT-LESS twin of `rlElemStructRow`: same three rungs in the same order, and the nominal
// floor `rlElemStructRow` grew for D32 (`a name that is a registered union VARIANT and not a
// registered plain struct has NO struct-table row`) went on ONE of the two copies. The
// structural rung under it then bridged `Circle`'s SHAPE onto `Dot`'s row and handed the
// loop var `(ref $Dot)` while the element read yields `(ref $uVarHeap[Circle])`.
//
// Gating the second copy puts both spellings back on the floor. The message differs from
// D50's own `field access but no struct type declared` because with the twin declared the
// receiver resolution gets one rung further before declining — both are located emit
// rejects, and WHICH one is what this file holds still.
//
// THE ONE-LINE CONTROL IS THE LAST TYPE DECL: delete `type Dot` and this program is D50's
// message instead. With `Sq`/`Shape` deleted — `Circle` a plain struct — it RUNS.

type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Dot = { r: i32 }

function cell(): i32 {
  const c: Circle = { r: 7 }
  const l: Circle[] = [c]
  let t = 0
  for v in l { t = t + v.r }
  return t
}

print(cell())
